1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,165
Two numbers, one target, and an interviewer watching your complexity.

2
00:00:05,115 --> 00:00:09,010
Let's go from n squared to a single pass in under three minutes.

3
00:00:19,268 --> 00:00:23,040
We get an array of distinct integers and a target sum.

4
00:00:22,990 --> 00:00:27,197
If any two numbers add up to the target, return them as a pair.

5
00:00:27,197 --> 00:00:30,646
If no pair exists, return an empty array.

6
00:00:34,412 --> 00:00:38,041
Our running example is three, five, minus four, eight,

7
00:00:38,041 --> 00:00:41,670
eleven, one, minus one, six, with a target of ten.

8
00:00:41,620 --> 00:00:44,222
The integers are distinct, negatives are allowed,

9
00:00:44,222 --> 00:00:46,824
and a number cannot pair with itself.

10
00:00:50,588 --> 00:00:54,336
The brute force checks every pair, which is n squared time.

11
00:00:54,286 --> 00:00:58,625
Sorting plus two pointers gets n log n with constant extra space.

12
00:00:58,625 --> 00:01:01,017
But we can do better on time.

13
00:01:04,772 --> 00:01:06,729
Here is the insight.

14
00:01:06,679 --> 00:01:10,537
For each number x, its partner is target minus x.

15
00:01:10,537 --> 00:01:13,290
A hash set of the values we have already

16
00:01:13,290 --> 00:01:16,042
seen answers "have I met the partner?" in constant time.

17
00:01:17,596 --> 00:01:19,998
So we walk the array once.

18
00:01:19,948 --> 00:01:23,133
Check whether target minus x is in the set.

19
00:01:23,133 --> 00:01:25,633
If it is, return the pair.

20
00:01:25,633 --> 00:01:28,458
Otherwise, add x and keep going.

21
00:01:32,212 --> 00:01:34,422
At three we need seven.

22
00:01:34,372 --> 00:01:37,292
Not seen yet, so we remember three.

23
00:01:38,844 --> 00:01:41,992
Five, minus four, and eight all miss their partners,

24
00:01:41,992 --> 00:01:45,140
so the set grows to three, five, minus four, eight.

25
00:01:46,652 --> 00:01:50,052
At eleven we need minus one, not there yet.

26
00:01:50,002 --> 00:01:51,949
One goes in too.

27
00:01:51,949 --> 00:01:55,891
Then at minus one we need eleven, and eleven is in the set.

28
00:01:55,891 --> 00:01:58,643
We return eleven and minus one.

29
00:02:02,396 --> 00:02:08,242
In Java: create a HashSet of integers, loop over the array, and if the set contains target minus x, return

30
00:02:08,242 --> 00:02:14,089
a new array of target minus x and x. Otherwise add x. Falling out of the loop returns an empty array.

31
00:02:17,804 --> 00:02:21,179
We touch each number once, so time is O of n.

32
00:02:21,129 --> 00:02:25,060
The set can hold up to n values, so space is O of n.

33
00:02:28,820 --> 00:02:32,906
Edge cases: no pair means an empty array, checking before adding means

34
00:02:32,906 --> 00:02:36,991
a number never pairs with itself, and negatives work exactly the same way.

35
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:43,077
Recap: for each number, ask whether its partner has already appeared.

36
00:02:43,027 --> 00:02:46,669
The code is in the AlgoExpert repo, linked below.

37
00:02:46,669 --> 00:02:49,566
I'm Waqar, see you in the next one.

